, -0.0028629302978515625, -0.01763916015625, -0.01450347900390625, 0.00980377197265625, -0.001201629638671875, -0.008758544921875, -0.0013790130615234375, 0.016632080078125, -0.039520263671875, -0.014801025390625, -0.0018339157104492188, 0.0160064697265625, 0.0160980224609375, -0.0247955322265625, -0.005931854248046875, -0.0033969879150390625, 0.005458831787109375, 0.0015277862548828125, 0.0027713775634765625, -0.032501220703125, 0.0416259765625, -0.0168914794921875, -0.01148223876953125, -0.018341064453125, 0.006847381591796875, 0.0080718994140625, -0.0239715576171875, 0.018524169921875, 0.0298614501953125, 0.0148773193359375, 0.00157928466796875, -0.00601959228515625, -0.00372314453125, -0.0049285888671875, -0.0173492431640625, 0.01158905029296875, 0.037933349609375, -0.00391387939453125, 0.01494598388671875, -0.032379150390625, -0.004367828369140625, 0.0225372314453125, 0.0024013519287109375, 0.019989013671875, 0.003925323486328125, 0.005138397216796875, -0.01320648193359375, 0.0026035308837890625, 0.0275726318359375, -0.0256500244140625, -0.0157012939453125, 0.0103302001953125, -0.0005397796630859375, 0.005641937255859375, -0.018585205078125, 0.01412200927734375, -0.0362548828125, 0.01544952392578125, 0.00670623779296875, -0.003215789794921875, 0.031341552734375, -0.0214996337890625, 0.00995635986328125, -0.016265869140625, -0.0233917236328125, 0.012847900390625, -0.005596160888671875, -0.00695037841796875, -0.014007568359375, -0.020599365234375, 0.01439666748046875, -0.015869140625, -0.006496429443359375, -0.026641845703125, -0.0005021095275878906, -0.01165008544921875, -0.0251007080078125, 0.0257110595703125, 0.00997161865234375, -0.049041748046875, 0.01284027099609375, 0.0013418197631835938, 0.0108795166015625, -0.00638580322265625, 0.0039825439453125, 0.035888671875, -0.0028018951416015625, 0.017425537109375, 0.001964569091796875, 0.0022296905517578125, -0.02349853515625, 0.036102294921875, -0.021209716796875, -0.0017108917236328125, -0.035858154296875, -0.032989501953125, -0.01580810546875, -0.02203369140625, 0.040252685546875, 0.028961181640625, 0.019805908203125, 0.0108489990234375, 0.023406982421875, 0.006328582763671875, 0.00933837890625, -0.04962158203125, 0.00572967529296875, -0.00017964839935302734, 0.001369476318359375, 0.030670166015625, -0.0277557373046875, 0.006580352783203125, -0.0208282470703125, -0.01251220703125, -0.0033092498779296875, 0.0091094970703125, 0.016265869140625, 0.0146636962890625, -0.003692626953125, 0.0098876953125, -0.01528167724609375, 0.01806640625, -0.0184478759765625, 0.02392578125, -0.005809783935546875, 0.0127716064453125, 0.01141357421875, -0.026123046875, -0.005962371826171875, -0.0110321044921875, -0.0272369384765625, -0.016326904296875, -0.01129150390625, 0.0165557861328125, -0.040618896484375, 0.0218658447265625, 0.005069732666015625, 0.0150909423828125, -0.01004791259765625, -0.00589752197265625, -0.01474761962890625, 0.01528167724609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In this context, we define **level-0 actions** as the choices that a player would make when reasoning as a level-0 player (i.e., without strategic consideration of others), and **level-0 beliefs** as participants\u2019 explicit predictions or descriptions of what a level-0 player would do in a given game. This study investigates two main research questions: (1) Do level-0 actions and beliefs coincide in classic experimental games? (2) How are level-0 behaviors perceived by others? We hypothesize that the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs is context-dependent and that deviations from standard level-0 assumptions can be systematically observed.\n\nTo address these questions, we apply the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which elicits both actions and beliefs from groups of participants. In our implementation, teams of three to five participants were randomly assigned and instructed to discuss each game until they reached a consensus on two points: (a) the action they would take as a level-0 player, and (b) their belief about what a generic level-0 player would do. Individual initial responses were recorded before discussion, and group consensus was reached through open discussion, with a majority rule applied if unanimity was not possible. This procedure allows for a more precise identification of level-0 reasoning by separating individual intuition from group consensus.\n\n**Results: Beauty Contest Game**\n\nIn the canonical beauty contest game, where players choose numbers in an attempt to guess a fraction of the average choice, Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) found that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard assumption that level-0 players choose randomly or use salient focal points. Our study replicates this result: in our sample (N = 60 teams), 87% of elicited level-0 beliefs matched the corresponding level-0 actions (95% CI: 78%\u201393%), and both clustered around the salient focal point of zero, consistent with the standard model. A chi-squared test confirmed that the alignment was significantly greater than chance (p < 0.001). This strong alignment reinforces the validity of the traditional level-0 assumption in this context.\n\n**Results: 11-20 Game Variant**\n\nIn contrast, we study a variant of the 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), in which players select integers from 11 to 20 and the highest unique number wins a prize. Our variant modifies the payoff structure by doubling the reward for choosing the highest unique number (from 10 to 20 points), thereby increasing the incentive for strategic reasoning and making the stakes for successful level-k reasoning more pronounced. Goeree et al. (2018) observed that behavior in this game often deviates from standard level-k predictions, with players not following the iterative reasoning process assumed by the model. In our experiment (N = 60 teams), only 54% of elicited level-0 beliefs matched observed level-0 actions (95% CI: 41%\u201366%), and both were widely dispersed across the available choices rather than clustering on a single focal point. This divergence from the standard level-0 assumption suggests that players may use alternative heuristics or focal points not captured by the traditional model, such as favoring the endpoints or avoiding the most obvious choices. Qualitative analysis of team discussions revealed frequent references to \u201cchoosing the lowest\u201d or \u201cchoosing the highest\u201d as plausible level-0 strategies, and some teams explicitly rejected randomization or focal points as unrealistic. Our findings thus extend the results of Goeree et al. (2018) by showing that heterogeneity in level-0 reasoning, rather than just higher-level strategic thinking, can explain the observed behavioral patterns. This is consistent with alternative models of level-0 behavior that allow for context-dependent heuristics (e.g., Crawford et al., 2013; Wright & Leyton-Brown, 2014).\n\n**Discussion and Implications**\n\nOur results demonstrate that the correspondence between level-0 beliefs and actions is highly context-dependent: it holds in the beauty contest game but breaks down in the 11-20 game variant. This challenges the assumption that level-0 behavior is stable across games and highlights the need for level-k models to incorporate game-specific factors, such as the salience of focal points, payoff structures, and the complexity of available choices. We suggest that future models should allow for flexible specifications of level-0 behavior that can be empirically calibrated for each game, for example by incorporating observed distributions of level-0 choices or by allowing for multiple candidate heuristics whose weights are estimated from data, as in the cognitive hierarchy model (Camerer et al., 2004) or the quantal response equilibrium framework (McKelvey & Palfrey, 1995).\n\nIn summary, our study shows that identifying level-0 reasoning requires careful consideration of both beliefs and actions, and that deviations from standard assumptions are informative about the heuristics players use. These insights can guide the refinement of behavioral models and improve predictions in experimental and applied settings."}}